// Polygons are the Commons drawing's shapes on a 3 by 5 grid, with repeated points removed.
vexyl 1
meta {
  name "Rubber Pride Flag"
  designer "Peter Tolos"
  designer "Scott Moats"
  encoder "Naomi Persephone Amethyst"
  license "CC-BY-SA-3.0"
  adapted-from "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubber_Fetish_Pride_Flag.svg" {
    author "RayneVanDunem"
    license "CC-BY-SA-3.0"
    part "the zigzag bands"
  }
  source "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubber_and_PVC_fetishism"
  source "https://crwflags.com/fotw/flags/qq-rubr.html"
  note "The designers' own publication was not found. Wikipedia credits Scott Moats and Peter Tolos with the flag in 1995 (an infobox on a related page says 1994) and quotes their color meanings through a Marie Claire article: black for lust for the look and feel of shiny black rubber, red for blood passion for rubber and rubbermen, yellow for drive for intense rubber play and fantasies. No date is given here because the sources disagree."
  note "FOTW describes the flag as a black field with a wide gold zigzag stripe outlined in red about a third of the way from the top, and a thin red stripe following a parallel path about a quarter of that width below. It also gives the Commons colors as #d32011 red and #fcc800 yellow and says real flags often use lighter shades, which is unverified."
  note "All geometry and the three colors (black, #fcc800, #d32011) are the Commons drawing's, which its author calls a personal rendition of a public-domain flag by the two designers. The stripes are kinked bands with no name in the format, so they are written as the drawing's polygons: a yellow band, a black cut that leaves it outlined, and red outlines. The 3:5 proportion is the drawing's."
}
palette {
  black "#000000"
  yellow "#fcc800"
  red "#d32011"
}
proportion hoist=3 fly=5

field black

charge polygon yellow {
  point fly=3.3896 hoist=2.1624
  point fly=2.9477 hoist=2.3876
  point fly=2.6622 hoist=1.9798
  point fly=2.1073 hoist=1.1875
  point fly=0 hoist=1.1875
